% =======================================================
\section{Continuous Magnetic Hard-Iron Correction}
\label{sec:mag-hard-iron}

The startup alignment of Sec.~\ref{sec:mag-two-stage} establishes a magnetic
world reference and therefore fixes the yaw gauge.  A body-fixed magnetometer
offset biases that gauge even when the attitude tracker itself is stable.  The
deployed correction is consequently formulated as a continuously running,
measurement-only estimator of the additive magnetic offset.  Its output changes
the magnetometer measurement model; it does not write an attitude state or reset
Kalman covariance.

\subsection{Why the standing error is a gauge error}
\label{sec:mag-gauge}

Let $\mat A_i$ be the yaw-stripped body-to-level rotation supplied by the
private Mahony proxy at magnetometer sample $i$, $\vct B_\ell$ the field in that
level frame, and $\vct b_m$ a body-fixed additive offset.  The raw measurement
is
\begin{equation}
\vct m_i=\mat A_i^{\top}\vct B_\ell+\vct b_m+\vct n_i .
\label{eq:mag-offset-model}
\end{equation}
A startup acquisition that averages the rotated field obtains
\begin{equation}
\bar{\vct w}
=\frac{1}{N}\sum_i\mat A_i\vct m_i
=\vct B_\ell+\bar{\mat A}\vct b_m,
\qquad
\bar{\mat A}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_i\mat A_i .
\label{eq:mag-ref-average}
\end{equation}
For a vessel that maintains approximately one heading,
$\bar{\mat A}\approx\mat I$ and the learned reference therefore points along
$\vct B_\ell+\vct b_m$, not the true field.  Once that direction has defined
north, every internally consistent attitude update preserves the same gauge
error.  A longer startup average reduces random noise but cannot separate the
offset from the world field at a single attitude.

Eliminating $\vct B_\ell$ from the weighted normal equations makes the
identifiability condition explicit,
\begin{equation}
\underbrace{(\mat I-\bar{\mat A}^{\top}\bar{\mat A})}_{\mat M}\vct b_m
=\bar{\vct m}-\bar{\mat A}^{\top}\bar{\vct w} .
\label{eq:mag-normal}
\end{equation}
Small roll and pitch variations make $\mat M$ nonzero, but for only a few
degrees of excitation its smallest eigenvalues remain small.  The limitation is
therefore conditioning, not sample count, which is why the estimator keeps the
window open after startup.

\subsection{Exogenous continuous offset estimator}
\label{sec:mag-hi-estimator}

Exponentially weighted sufficient statistics of $(\mat A_i,\vct m_i)$ are
maintained for the lifetime of the record with an effective memory of
\SI{600}{s}; the offset solve is updated once per second.  The rotation comes
from the yaw-stripped Mahony proxy and the magnetic input is the \emph{raw}
measurement before any estimated offset is subtracted.  Thus neither the MEKF
state nor the estimator's own corrected stream is fed back into the fit.

Because scale, cross-axis, and misalignment errors are not part of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:mag-offset-model}, an unregularized inverse of the weakly excited
$\mat M$ can alias those distortions into a spurious hard-iron estimate.  The
deployed solve therefore uses an excitation-scaled ridge,
\begin{equation}
\left[\mat M+\left(\rho_0+ho_1\frac{\operatorname{tr}\mat M}{3}\right)
\mat I\right]\hat{\vct b}_m
=\bar{\vct m}-\bar{\mat A}^{\top}\bar{\vct w},
\label{eq:mag-ridge}
\end{equation}
with $\rho_0=4\times10^{-3}$ and $\rho_1=0.5$.  The excitation-dependent term
prevents a large sea from being interpreted as automatically providing a
cleaner sensor model: it provides more geometric information, but the
unmodeled distortion grows through the same geometry.

An estimate is offered only after at least \num{500} effective samples, when
$N\lambda_{\min}(\mat M)\ge2$, when the fitted offset is below \pct{35} of the
measured field norm, and when the weighted residual of the level-frame model is
no greater than \SI{3}{\micro\tesla} RMS.  These gates reject insufficient
excitation and obvious model failure; they cannot detect the part of soft-iron
or misalignment error that the additive model explains successfully.

\subsection{Application without an attitude reset}
\label{sec:mag-hi-apply}

Subtracting the same offset from both the measurement and the learned magnetic
reference would preserve the existing innovation and therefore preserve the
wrong yaw gauge.  The implementation instead slews the offset subtraction into
the raw magnetometer stream with a \SI{45}{s} time constant while retaining the
canonical reference direction established by acquisition.  Only the reference
magnitude and dip are adjusted by the delta implied by the applied offset.  The
ordinary magnetometer Kalman update then moves yaw toward the newly consistent
field direction.

No attitude state is written and no covariance is reset.  Once startup
acquisition is complete, the hard-iron correction is therefore a bounded change
of measurement-model parameters inside the normal Live recursion rather than a
hybrid reinitialization event.  If the estimator never validates, the
measurement stream and reference remain unchanged.

\subsection{Limitations of the additive model}
\label{sec:mag-hi-limits}

The estimator identifies only a body-fixed additive offset.  It cannot in
general separate hard iron from soft-iron scale/cross-axis error or sensor
misalignment using the fixed-heading directional records of this study.  The
aliased part of those distortions can satisfy the residual gate because it is,
by construction, the part the additive model explains.  Multi-heading
excitation is required to identify a full distortion model; the current
implementation instead stands down when hull turning violates its residual
assumptions.

This failure mode is measured rather than assumed away.  Across additional
magnetometer-calibration draws, cases whose standing error is
misalignment-dominated can become worse when the additive correction is
applied, even though the shipped draw is strongly offset dominated.  The ridge
in Eq.~\eqref{eq:mag-ridge} is therefore a bound on model misspecification, not
merely numerical regularization.  The quantitative deterministic result for the
reported draw is presented with the other empirical observations in
Sec.~\ref{sec:mag-hi-results}.
